  abstract = {Sensitivity of an oligotropic lake planktonic bacterial community to oxygen stress.Dissolved O2 at .apprx. 4 times normal saturation (42 mg l-1) inhibited the growth and metabolism of summer planktonic bacteria in the surface water alpine oligotrophic Mountain Lake (Giles County, Virginia, USA). Data were derived from growth of colony-forming units (CFU) on membrane filters, D-[U-14C]glucose incorporation into the extractable lipid of these CFU, and respiration and assimilation of D-[U-14C]glucose by lake water samples. Statistically significant (.alpha. {$<$} 0.05) differences were not detected in CFU or 14C incorporation in lipid when superoxide dismutase (30 U ml-1) or catalase (130 .+-. ml-1) was added to the medium. Thus, exogenous O byproducts apparently are not responsible for the observed inhibition of growth and metabolism.}
